  "body": "I got here from http://www.w3c-test.org/workers/interfaces/WorkerUtils/importScripts/002.html which asserts that in this situation:\n\n```\nvar ran = false;\nvar threw = false;\nvar code = undefined;\ntry {\n  importScripts('data:text/javascript,ran=true','http://foo bar');\n} catch(e) {\n  threw = true;\n  code = e.code;\n}\npostMessage([ran, threw, code]);\n```\n\nthe posted message will be `[false, true, 12]`.  In particular, it asserts that the space in the second URL means the script at the first URL did NOT run.  But as far as I can tell from the spec, the spec fetches and runs each script before doing anything with the next one.\n\nIs the test wrong?  Is the spec wrong?  Firefox seems to \"pass\" the `ran` part of the test (doesn't run the first script).  Chrome \"fails\" it (as in, does what I think the spec says).  Safari \"passes\".  IE Edge complains about a `SyntaxError` on the `<!--` the test starts with so I have no idea how it handles importScripts so far.\n",
